{"created": "2026-11-11T20:53:47.448696+00:00", "modified": "2026-11-11T20:53:47.661148+00:00", "id": 24146118, "conceptrecid": "24146117", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.24146118", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.24146117", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24146118", "metadata": {"title": "plate-novae", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.24146118", "publication_date": "2026-11-11", "description": "<p>This repository searches the Harvard DASCH plates (about 1890 to 1990) for earlier eruptions of known novae, which would show that a nova is recurrent.</p><p>Status: pilot, claimed, not peer reviewed. No person outside the project has checked it.</p><p>A pilot on five stars reports, as claimed and not checked by a person, that 5 of 17 known eruptions of T Pyx, U Sco and RS Oph from 1890 to 1989 were recovered (6 of 17 with a fainter quiet level). An RS Oph signal in March 1914 is an unverified flag, not a discovery.</p><p>As <a href=\"https://dasch.cfa.harvard.edu/citing/\">DASCH asks</a>: This work has made use of data provided by Digital Access to a Sky Century @ Harvard (DASCH), which has been partially supported by NSF grants AST-0407380, AST-0909073, and AST-1313370. Work on DASCH Data Release 7 received support from the Smithsonian American Women's History Initiative Pool.</p><p>Raw DASCH light curves are not redistributed here and are not in the archive. The scripts fetch them.</p><p>Directed by Ahraz Arifuddin</p><p>Researched and verified by different model families across Gemini, Claude, Grok, OpenAI, and Muse</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Arifuddin, Ahraz", "affiliation": null}], "keywords": ["astronomy", "dasch", "foundation0", "novae", "photographic-plates", "variable-stars"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "https://github.com/Foundation-0/plate-novae", "relation": "isSupplementTo", "resource_type": "software", "scheme": "url"}], "version": "v1.0.0", "custom": {"code:codeRepository": "https://github.com/Foundation-0/plate-novae"}, "resou